  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Compute optimal policy for discrete MDP using alternating policy evaluation and greedy policy improvement.\n\nPolicy iteration guarantees strictly monotonic improvement in state values at every iteration, converging to global optimal policy in finite steps.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nV^{\\pi} = (\\mathbf{I} - \\gamma \\mathbf{P}^{\\pi})^{-1} \\mathbf{R}^{\\pi}, \\quad \\pi'(s) = \\arg\\max_a \\left[ R(s, a) + \\gamma \\sum_{s'} P(s'|s, a) V^{\\pi}(s') \\right]\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Solve linear system for state values under current stationary policy pi\n2. Greedily update action at each state maximizing expected 1-step Q-value\n3. Terminate when policy is stable and return optimal action indices\n\n### Returns\nList of integers representing optimal action choice per state.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Policy Iteration\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn reinforcement learning, there is no supervisor providing correct answers. Instead, an agent learns by trial and error: taking actions, observing environment states, and receiving feedback rewards. The agent must continuously balance exploiting known high-reward actions with exploring new actions that might be even better. **Policy Iteration** implements a core decision-making or value-estimation algorithm.\n\nPolicy iteration guarantees strictly monotonic improvement in state values at every iteration, converging to global optimal policy in finite steps. In this problem, the goal is to: compute optimal policy for discrete mdp using alternating policy evaluation and greedy policy improvement.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nV^{\\pi} = (\\mathbf{I} - \\gamma \\mathbf{P}^{\\pi})^{-1} \\mathbf{R}^{\\pi}, \\quad \\pi'(s) = \\arg\\max_a \\left[ R(s, a) + \\gamma \\sum_{s'} P(s'|s, a) V^{\\pi}(s') \\right]\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Solve linear system for state values under current stationary policy pi\n2. **Phase 2**: Greedily update action at each state maximizing expected 1-step Q-value\n3. **Phase 3**: Terminate when policy is stable and return optimal action indices\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Bellman Equations**: The core idea of dynamic programming in RL is that the value of a state equals the immediate reward plus the discounted expected value of the next state.\n* **Sample Efficiency**: Replay buffers and temporal difference updates allow agents to learn stably from past experiences.\n* **Policy Optimization**: Whether using value-based methods (like Q-learning) or policy gradients (like PPO), algorithms converge toward the optimal behavioral policy.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: P = [[[0.7, 0.3], [0.1, 0.9]], [[0.2, 0.8], [0.8, 0.2]]], R = [[5.0, 10.0], [-1.0, 2.0]], gamma = 0.9\n\nOutput: [1, 1]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated rl_policy_iteration under standard test conditions."
